// audit-ci configuration for TaskBuddy CI (task A6).
//
// Runs `npm audit` and FAILS the build on any advisory at moderate severity or
// above that is NOT in the allowlist below. This turns `npm audit` into a
// meaningful gate (it catches NEW vulnerabilities) instead of permanent red
// noise from known dev/build-only advisories.
//
// Usage in CI (add to .github/workflows/ci.yml after the build step):
//   - name: Security audit (allowlisted)
//     run: npx --yes audit-ci@^7 --config ./audit-ci.jsonc
//
// The allowlist mirrors the "Accepted" dependency findings (DEP-02..DEP-06) in
// .do_not_upload/security_assessment.md. DEP-02..DEP-05 are dev/build/test only
// and not reachable in the running service. DEP-01 (nodemailer runtime advisory,
// GHSA-p6gq-j5cr-w38f) was FIXED (nodemailer@^9), NOT allowlisted — if it ever
// regresses, CI must fail. DEP-07 (sharp <0.35 inherited libvips CVEs,
// GHSA-f88m-g3jw-g9cj, high) was likewise FIXED, NOT allowlisted: sharp bumped to
// ^0.35.3 in backend + frontend, with a root `overrides.sharp` so next's optional copy
// collapses to the patched version too. sharp decodes untrusted child-uploaded evidence
// photos (backend/src/services/storage.ts) — runtime-reachable, so it must be patched,
// never ignored. DEP-06 (GHSA-8988, otel baggage) was a runtime risk acceptance until
// @sentry/node 10 fixed it (2026-10-27). No runtime acceptance is left: DEP-19 (zod) became
// build-tooling only when backend and web moved to zod 4 (details by its entry below).
//
// RE-TRIAGE: remove an entry here when a non-downgrade patch lands upstream, or
// when an advisory escalates to critical / moves into the runtime path. Keep
// this file and security_assessment.md (§ Dependency Audit) in sync.
{
  // Fail on moderate, high, and critical advisories (anything not allowlisted).
  "moderate": true,

  "allowlist": [
    // DEP-02 — serialize-javascript RCE, via workbox-build -> next-pwa@5 (PWA BUILD only).
    "GHSA-5c6j-r48x-rmvq",

    // DEP-03 (postcss XSS, GHSA-qx2v-qp2m-jg93) REMOVED 2026-07-23 — FIXED, not allowlisted.
    // postcss bumped to ^8.5.22 in frontend + a root `overrides.postcss` so next's vendored copy
    // collapses to the patched version too. The bump was forced by GHSA-6g55-p6wh-862q (high,
    // CVSS 7.5: arbitrary file read / info disclosure via an attacker-controlled sourceMappingURL
    // in a CSS comment, range <=8.5.11), published after this repo's last green run on main.
    // npm's own "fix" for it was next@9.3.3 — a multi-major DOWNGRADE — so the override is the
    // correct route. 8.5.22 clears both advisories, which is why the old XSS entry goes with it:
    // per the note above, a dead allowlist entry is not harmless.

    // DEP-04 — minimatch ReDoS x3, via @typescript-eslint -> eslint-config-next@14 (LINT only).
    "GHSA-3ppc-4f35-3m26",
    "GHSA-7r86-cg39-jmmj",
    "GHSA-23c5-xmqv-rm74",

    // DEP-05 (js-yaml DoS, GHSA-h67p-54hq-rp68) REMOVED 2026-07-21 — the advisory no longer
    // appears in `npm audit`, so the entry was dead. A dead entry is not harmless: it would
    // silently suppress the same advisory if a future dependency reintroduced it.

    // DEP-06 (@opentelemetry/core baggage, GHSA-8988, runtime) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED by
    // @sentry/node 10.76.2, which brings @opentelemetry/core 2.12.0.

    // DEP-08 (brace-expansion DoS, GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg) REMOVED 2026-08-05 — FIXED, not
    // allowlisted. The entry's own re-triage condition was "remove as soon as a patched
    // brace-expansion ships and the dependency tree can reach it", and both halves are now true:
    // 1.1.18, 2.1.4 and 5.0.9 all carry the fix, and `npm update brace-expansion` pulled every one
    // of the tree's three major lines onto them. The accepted risk it recorded — a runtime-reachable
    // path via @sentry/node -> minimatch -> brace-expansion — is therefore gone rather than tolerated.
    // Per the note at the top of this file, a dead entry is not harmless: left here it would silently
    // suppress the same advisory if a future dependency reintroduced a vulnerable copy.
    //
    // The successor advisory GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 ("bypassing the CVE-2026-14257 mitigation") is
    // cleared by the same versions and is likewise NOT allowlisted.

    // DEP-09 (uuid GHSA-w5hq via xcode) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED. @expo/config-plugins 57.0.11
    // moved to xcode 4, which no longer depends on uuid. Mobile runtime fingerprint unchanged.

    // DEP-10 — image-size: infinite-loop DoS in the ICNS parser (GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr) and in the
    // JXL/HEIF parsers (GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq). Both high, range <=2.0.2. Surfaced 2026-08-07.
    //
    // NO FIX EXISTS — not "no compatible fix", none at all: 2.0.2 is the latest published version
    // and the advisory range covers every release ever cut. An override has nothing to point at.
    // npm's suggested remedy is expo@53.0.27, a four-major DOWNGRADE from SDK 57.
    //
    // NOT REACHABLE, for reasons that are independent:
    //   1. BUILD-TIME ONLY. The sole consumer is metro@0.84.4, the React Native bundler —
    //      `npm ls image-size --all` shows exactly one path. Metro runs on the build machine, is
    //      never bundled into the shipped app, and is absent from the backend and web frontend.
    //   2. THE INPUTS ARE OURS. Both advisories need a malformed ICNS/JXL/HEIF file. Metro only
    //      measures images committed to this repo. No user, child or attacker can hand it one.
    //      Child-uploaded evidence photos are decoded by sharp, in the backend, on another machine
    //      — a different library on a different host, and it is patched (see DEP-07 above).
    //   3. The worst case is therefore a hung EAS build, not a production outage.
    //
    // RE-TRIAGE: remove when image-size publishes a release above 2.0.2 carrying the fix, or when
    // metro drops it. Re-examine immediately if image-size ever gains a consumer outside metro.
    "GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr",
    "GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq",

    // DEP-11 (deepmerge-ts GHSA-ggr8 via the prisma CLI) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED by Prisma 7.

    // DEP-12 (qs GHSA-4mjr-xmp4-gh2g + GHSA-x5fp-wj9c-mxmx, runtime) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED, not
    // allowlisted. express 4.22.3 depends on qs ~6.16.0, the re-triage condition this entry named;
    // backend bumped to express ^4.22.3 and every qs in the tree is now 6.16.1. If either advisory
    // ever fails this gate again, a dependency has pulled an old qs back in: fix it, do not re-add.

    // DEP-13 — decode-uri-component: DoS via exponential decoding of a crafted URI
    // (GHSA-vcc3-ghjq-m6fr, moderate, range <=0.4.2). **This is the advisory that has been failing
    // CI on `main` since 2026-08-31**, i.e. every build since #191, on a tree nobody had changed.
    //
    // Exactly one path, and it is the mobile app only:
    //     mobile -> expo-router@57.0.9 -> query-string@7.1.3 -> decode-uri-component@0.2.2
    // `npm ls query-string --all` confirms expo-router is its only consumer.
    //
    // REACHABLE IN PRINCIPLE, and worth being precise about rather than waving away: expo-router
    // parses incoming URLs at runtime, and Android App Links are live and verified, so a crafted
    // https://gettaskbuddy.com/... link could reach this parser. What that buys an attacker is the
    // app becoming unresponsive on the device of someone who chose to open their link. No data is
    // exposed, nothing executes, no other user is affected, and the backend is untouched — express
    // does not use query-string.
    //
    // NO FIX AVAILABLE: npm's own suggested remedy is expo-router@5.1.11, a FIVE-major downgrade
    // from the SDK 57 router, which would take the whole mobile app with it. query-string@7.1.3 is
    // what expo-router pins; 8.x dropped the dependency, but expo-router has not moved.
    //
    // RE-TRIAGE: remove when expo-router depends on query-string >= 8 (or drops it), which is the
    // upstream fix. Re-examine immediately if decode-uri-component ever gains a consumer in the
    // backend or the web frontend, where the exposure would be per-request rather than per-tap.
    "GHSA-vcc3-ghjq-m6fr",

    // ---- 2026-10-08 batch (DEP-14..DEP-18), owner-approved 2026-10-08 ---------------------------
    // 33 advisories landed between the last green run on main (2026-09-16) and 2026-10-08. Every
    // RUNTIME one was FIXED, not listed here: next 16.3.4 -> 16.3.8 (next/og RCE, image-optimizer
    // SSRF, cache poisoning), proxy-addr 2.0.8 (critical, trust-proxy IP spoofing, which feeds the
    // rate limiter), engine.io 6.6.11, compression 1.8.2, sharp 0.35.5, handlebars 4.7.10,
    // shell-quote 1.12.0, source-map-js 1.2.2, fast-uri 3.1.8, brace-expansion, and
    // nodemailer 9 -> 10.0.16 (five addressparser/DNS advisories; v10's only break is Node >= 20,
    // the VPS runs 22). `npm audit fix` itself is unusable here: it dies on a pre-existing mobile
    // peer conflict (jest-expo vs @react-native/jest-preset), so fixes went in via targeted
    // `npm update <pkg>`. The five below have no usable fix.

    // DEP-14 - node-forge RSA signature check accepts extra nested data (GHSA-86w9-cpqp-85rv, high).
    // Mobile BUILD tooling only: @expo/cli and expo-updates -> @expo/code-signing-certificates.
    // Not in the app bundle, and expo-updates code signing is not configured in app.config.ts.
    // 1.4.0 is the latest node-forge; npm's "fix" is expo@44, a 13-major downgrade.
    // RE-TRIAGE: when node-forge > 1.4.0 ships, or if update code signing is ever turned on.
    "GHSA-86w9-cpqp-85rv",

    // DEP-15 (sprintf-js GHSA-hp3w) REMOVED 2026-10-27: no longer in the tree.

    // DEP-16 (otel instrumentation db.user, GHSA-qqmp) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED by @sentry/node 10.

    // DEP-17 (postcss-selector-parser GHSA-rj75) REMOVED 2026-10-27: FIXED, 6.1.5 via `npm update`.

    // DEP-18 (braces GHSA-vfj7) REMOVED 2026-10-27: no longer in the tree.

    // DEP-19 - zod memory exhaustion on large arrays (GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5, high).
    // NO LONGER RUNTIME (2026-10-27): backend and frontend moved to zod 4.6.7, which is patched.
    // The only zod 3 left is mobile BUILD/LINT tooling: @expo/cli (validates our app config) and
    // eslint-config-expo -> eslint-plugin-react-hooks (validates lint options). Neither sees a
    // request or user input. Was an owner-approved runtime acceptance from 2026-10-26.
    // RE-TRIAGE: remove on Expo SDK 58 (Phase 6), or at once if zod 3 appears outside mobile tooling.
    "GHSA-mrhx-c7xc-m3w5"

    // nanoid (GHSA-2v37-7h3g-55p8, high, <3.3.17) surfaced alongside DEP-10 and is deliberately
    // NOT here — it was FIXED. `npm update nanoid` took the tree to 3.3.18. It was runtime-reachable
    // in the mobile app via expo-router, not merely a build dependency, so allowlisting it would
    // have been the wrong call even though the loop needs a custom generator to trigger.
  ]
}
